/// heap: the live view of the heap that the leak probes of test/fort and test/lsp share.
//
// A leak probe runs many identical rounds and holds an early sample of the
// heap against the late ones. A round that gives every block back leaves the
// heap where it was. A round that keeps a block moves it.
//
// The live view is the count of the bytes of the live allocations of the
// runtime, which std.heap.live_bytes gives. Each `new` and each `regrow` of
// the program takes its storage from that allocator (D10.9), so a leaked block
// is in the count, whatever its size and wherever the allocator put it. An
// allocation counts with the bytes that the allocator keeps for it
// (std.heap.kept_bytes): a block of 64 bytes counts 64, and a block of 1 byte
// counts 16. A `del` takes the same bytes out of the count at once, so a round
// that keeps nothing moves the view by 0 on each target.
//
// The view does not count an allocation of C: storage of `malloc` or of
// `calloc` is no storage of the runtime (D10.3). A probe of a path that keeps
// an owner of C needs another view.
//
// At d2f5b2b6 this module gave two more views, and no probe reads them now.
// The system view was the program break, which sbrk(0) reports. The block view
// was the address of a fresh 32-byte block. Each of them moved when only the
// order or the count of the allocations moved, with nothing kept.
// notes/testing.md gives the measurement.

import std.heap as allocator;

fn live_view() u64 {
    return allocator.live_bytes();
}

// How far apart two samples are, whichever way the heap moved. A leak that
// hands a later round a lower value is a leak too, and a subtraction the wrong
// way round traps before the assertion.
fn distance(u64 a, u64 b) u64 {
    if (a > b) {
        return a - b;
    }
    return b - a;
}
